# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Until these are set the app runs on the stub
# repository: real structure, no data, and the sign-in page says "Not configured".
#
# On Vercel these two live in the project's Environment Variables, not in a file — see
# "Deploying" in ../README.md. The VITE_ prefix is load-bearing and it is the FRAMEWORK's,
# not Supabase's: Vite exposes only VITE_-prefixed variables to client code, and Vercel's
# Supabase integration assumes Next.js unless told otherwise, so it provisions
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY — names `import.meta.env`
# cannot see at all. Tell the integration the framework is Vite and it writes these two.
#
# app/src/data/supabaseEnv.ts also reads VITE_SUPABASE_DATABASE_URL and
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY, the spellings the old Netlify Supabase extension used. That
# fallback is kept for the deployments that still carry those names; the two below win
# when both are set, and they are the ones to use.
#
# The key is the publishable one (`sb_publishable_…`) from Supabase's API settings.
# Never put the service role key here or anywhere else with a VITE_ prefix — it
# bypasses RLS, and a VITE_ variable is public the moment it is built.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
